.TH SYS_SHM_CREATE 2
.SH NAME
sys_shm_create, sys_shm_map, sys_shm_hold, sys_shm_close \- shared memory objects (SYS_SHM_CREATE, SYS_SHM_MAP, SYS_SHM_HOLD, SYS_SHM_CLOSE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_shm_create(uint64_t id, size_t size);
void *sys_shm_map(uint64_t id, void *addr, int flags);
long sys_shm_hold(uint64_t id, int pid);
long sys_shm_close(uint64_t id);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A shared memory object is a set of zeroed pages named by
.IR id ,
a random 63-bit number its creator chose (with
.BR sys_random (2)).
Knowing
.I id
is the right to use it: map it, hold it. It is how big data goes between
processes without copies (the desktop's windows, the POSIX layer's
shared files and program images).
.PP
.B sys_shm_create
creates an object of
.I size
bytes (at most 256 MiB, counted in the creator's quota); the caller holds
it.
.B sys_shm_map
maps the whole object into the caller, with the rights in
.I flags
.RB ( PROT_READ ,
.BR PROT_WRITE ,
.BR PROT_EXEC ),
at
.I addr
with
.BR MAP_FIXED ,
else where the kernel chooses, as
.BR sys_mmap (2).
.B sys_shm_hold
makes process
.I pid
(0: the caller) hold it once more, so it outlives the creator;
.B sys_shm_close
makes the caller hold it once less.
.PP
An object lives while it is held or mapped; a process's holds end with
it. Unmapping is
.BR sys_munmap (2).
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sys_shm_map :
the address, or a negative error (cast to a pointer). The others: 0, or
a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EEXIST
.RB ( sys_shm_create )
The id is taken.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.RB ( sys_shm_create )
.I id
is 0 or has its top bit set, or
.I size
is 0 or above 256 MiB.
.RB ( sys_shm_map )
Bad flags or address.
.TP
.B ENOENT
No object
.I id
(or, for
.BR sys_shm_close ,
none the caller holds).
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No memory, or the quota is reached.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_mmap (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR sys_futex (2),
.BR sys_random (2)
